      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the signed curvature of the curve $y=2{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+1$ at $x=0.$",
      "worked_solution": "The signed curvature $k(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(x)=\\frac{{y}^{″}(x)}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=6{x}^{2}-2x,\\,{y}^{″}(x)=12x-2\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(x) & =\\frac{12x-2}{{[1+{(6{x}^{2}-2x)}^{2}]}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(0) & =\\frac{12(0)-2}{{[1+{(6\\cdot {0}^{2}-2\\cdot 0)}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{{1}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
