      "problem": "Find the radius of curvature for the curve $r(t)=2{e}^{t}\\,i+{e}^{2t}\\,j$ at $t=0.$",
      "worked_solution": "The curvature $κ(t)$ of a plane curve $r(t)=x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{|{x}^{′}(t){y}^{″}(t)-{y}^{′}(t){x}^{″}(t)|}{([{x}^{′}(t)]^{2}+[{y}^{′}(t)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nThe radius of curvature $R$ is defined as\n$R(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}.$\n\nAlong our curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t)=2{e}^{t},\\,y(t)={e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the derivatives of the components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =2{e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{′}(t) & =2{e}^{2t} \\\\ {x}^{″}(t) & =2{e}^{t},\\, & {y}^{″}(t) & =4{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{|2{e}^{t}\\cdot 4{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{2t}\\cdot 2{e}^{t}|}{{((2{e}^{t})^{2}+(2{e}^{2t})^{2})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{3t}}{{(4{e}^{2t}+4{e}^{4t})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{(1+{e}^{2t})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{1}{2{(1+{e}^{2(0)})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{(1+1)}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the radius of curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(0) & =\\frac{1}{κ(0)}=4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
