      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the signed curvature of the curve $y=x-{e}^{x}$ at $x=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "The signed curvature $k(x)$ of a plane curve $y=y(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nk(x)=\\frac{{y}^{″}(x)}{(1+[{y}^{′}(x)]^{2})^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${y}^{′}(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=1-{e}^{x},\\,{y}^{″}(x)=-{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the formula for the signed curvature, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(x) & =\\frac{-{e}^{x}}{{[1+{(1-{e}^{x})}^{2}]}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{(1+1+{e}^{2x}-2{e}^{x})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{(2+{e}^{2x}-2{e}^{x})}^{3/2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(1) & =-\\frac{{e}^{(1)}}{{(2+{e}^{2(1)}-2{e}^{(1)})}^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{e}{({e}^{2}-2e+2)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
